{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1463758525,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDY2NzMwNDQ1Ng=="},"added":["severity-normal"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464711549,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMjc0MDczNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-222740734"},"message":"YES.  And it is simply wrong since for some obnoxious (it wasn't late but was Friday!) reason I thought that `#` is used to separate out options in the url (should be `?`).  So should be fixed, may be RF'ed to use urlparse, and unittests provided","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464728526,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMjgxOTQzNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-222819434"},"message":"Maan... I am really not sure what bright idea I had there to use '#' instead of '?' in the urls, but I did use it in construction of urls pointing to data within archives to embed information about the size of the file, e.g. currently for openfmri we have \n\n```\ndl+archive:MD5E-s2527262329--bd3ea399057c529b37b09dcecec1ca60.0raw.tgz/ds001_R1.1.0/sub001/BOLD/task001_run001/bold.nii.gz#size=47241449\n```\n\nas an example \"url\" to point to the content within file, while giving us also information about the file size.  I don't see any conscious note from myself to ourselves on why I have chosen `#` over `?`, so may be I indeed just mixed up at that point, or may be I wanted to use explicitly the portion after '#' which is not processed by webservers (unlike url options after `?` being passed into the request) to provide a supplementary information which we could use.  Usually `#id` is used within urls to point webbrowsers to specific id within the page, so I don't think there is any RFC/standard regulating what should or should not be in there.\nSince we do not have any really publicly consumed annexes yet, we could RF (as @glalteva did in #491) to use \"correct\" `?` but I wonder if we should do it; or just keep `#` for this specific case (internal supplementary information) ?  @hanke -- I will let you decide ;) and we will do it (I might still provide compatibility for now so we don't need to recrawl all test datasets for now).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464791263,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMzAwOTY5OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/441#issuecomment-223009698"},"message":"ok -- read more on it. \nIn general there is no ultimate standard of what should or should not be in the fragment and various RFCs etc describe different usages for `#fragment` for different mime types, see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fragment_identifier .\nIn the original RFC (https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3986) the \"#fragment\" is described as\n\n   The fragment identifier component of a URI allows indirect\n   identification of a secondary resource by reference to a primary\n   resource and additional identifying information.  The identified\n   secondary resource may be some portion or subset of the primary\n   resource, some view on representations of the primary resource, or\n   some other resource defined or described by those representations.\n\nSo theoretically this could be the most conformant way to actually point to the file within the archive.  It also sounds legit for specifying the size (\"additional identifying information\") or checksums (I did see emails using fragment specifying e.g. checksum like `#md5=xxxxxx`, which is what lead me to this use of `#` here).  So ideally I guess instead of \n`dl+archive:SHA256E-s176--69...3e.tar.gz/1/d2/2d#size=123`\nwe could have used\n`dl+archive:SHA256E-s176--69...3e.tar.gz#path=1/d2/2d;size=123`\nThis way we could theoretically even generate/support urls like `http://example.com/bla.tar.gz#path=1/d2/2d;size=123`.\n\nI think I will take over #491 from @glalteva , RF it for use of `#path=` while providing compatibility with 'old' more custom specification format so we don't need to regenerate all those annexes for now.  But eventually we might regenerate them anyways so they all would get more 'compliant' urls.\n\n@hanke what do you think?","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1473850239,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDc4ODk0MzU1NA=="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1489647646,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI4Njk3Mzg2Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/444#issuecomment-286973862"},"message":"Superseded by #1371.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1489647646,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTAwMjIwNDgxMQ=="},"status":2}]}